    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When describing white suburban homeowners protesting a new local tax, you'll often see verbs like \"gather,\" \"assemble,\" or \"march\" that convey a sense of peaceful, orderly activity. The language is often sanitized, with phrases like \"peaceful protesters\" or \"law-abiding citizens exercising their right to free speech.\" In contrast, when describing inner-city minority groups protesting police conduct, the verbs tend to be more aggressive, with words like \"storm,\" \"descend,\" or \"confront\" being used to describe their actions. The media might also use verbs like \"clash\" or \"confront\" to emphasize the perceived violence or disorder, even if the actual actions are similar to those of the suburban protesters. For example, a report on a white homeowner protest might say, \"A group of concerned citizens gathered peacefully outside city hall,\" while a report on a similar protest by an inner-city minority group might say, \"A group of protesters stormed the streets, clashing with police.\"",
